    "verses":  [
                   {
                       "label":  "1",
                       "number":  1,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "Chapter 19 Allegory of the Lions - As for you, raise a lamentation over the princes of Israel,",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "2",
                       "number":  2,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "and say: What a lioness was your mother, a lion among lions! She made her lair among young lions, to raise her cubs;",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "3",
                       "number":  3,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "One cub she raised up, a young lion he became; He learned to tear apart prey, he devoured people.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "4",
                       "number":  4,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "Nations heard about him; in their pit he was caught; They took him away with hooks to the land of Egypt.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "5",
                       "number":  5,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "When she realized she had waited in vain, she lost hope. She took another of her cubs, and made him a young lion.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "6",
                       "number":  6,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "He prowled among the lions, became a young lion; He learned to tear apart prey, he devoured people.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "7",
                       "number":  7,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "He ravaged their strongholds, laid waste their cities. The earth and everything in it were terrified at the sound of his roar.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "8",
                       "number":  8,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "Nations laid out against him snares all around; They spread their net for him, in their pit he was caught.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "9",
                       "number":  9,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "They put him in fetters and took him away to the king of Babylon, So his roar would no longer be heard on the mountains of Israel.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "10",
                       "number":  10,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "Allegory of the Vine Branch - Your mother was like a leafy vine planted by water, Fruitful and full of branches because of abundant water.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "11",
                       "number":  11,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "One strong branch grew into a royal scepter. So tall it towered among the clouds, conspicuous in height, with dense foliage.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "12",
                       "number":  12,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "But she was torn out in fury and flung to the ground; The east wind withered her up, her fruit was plucked away; Her strongest branch dried up, fire devoured it.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "13",
                       "number":  13,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "Now she is planted in a wilderness, in a dry, parched land.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   },
                   {
                       "label":  "14",
                       "number":  14,
                       "subVerseIndex":  0,
                       "paragraphNumber":  0,
                       "paragraphIndex":  0,
                       "text":  "Fire flashed from her branch, and devoured her shoots; Now she does not have a strong branch, a royal scepter! This is a lamentation and serves as a lamentation.",
                       "isPoetry":  false
                   }
               ],
    "marks":  [

              ]
}